  <section id="theorems">
    <h2>Why it holds: six finite steps, none assuming a resonance peak or a particular algorithm</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">A · every processor</span>
        <h3>Processing cannot add distinguishability</h3>
        <p>For any test, detection rate − false-alarm rate ≤ TV(P₁, P₀), and for equal priors the best accuracy is ½ + TV/2. Any
        channel applied the same way under both hypotheses, a sub-aperture bank, a tomogram, a renderer, a trained network,
        contracts TV. So 95% found at 5% false alarms needs TV ≥ 0.9, and a proved TV below it excludes that target for
        every method at once.</p>
        <details><summary>Proof</summary><p>Split p₁ − p₀ into positive and negative parts of equal mass TV. A test 0 ≤ t ≤ 1
        integrates to at most TV against it; the indicator of the positive part attains it. A processor K pulls back to the test
        y ↦ E[t(K(y))], so it can do no better on K’s output.</p></details>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">B · the finite change</span>
        <h3>The exact Gaussian divergence, bounded</h3>
        <p>For proper complex Gaussians, KL = tr(C₀⁻¹C₁) − d − log det(C₀⁻¹C₁) + δm*C₀⁻¹δm. With E the whitened covariance
        change and its eigenvalues e<sub>j</sub> &gt; −1, KL = Σ(e<sub>j</sub> − log(1 + e<sub>j</sub>)) + δm*C₀⁻¹δm, and since
        e − log(1 + e) ≤ e²/(2 min(1 + e, 1)), for equal means (speckle is zero-mean; otherwise the mean term is added) and
        ‖E‖ &lt; 1, KL ≤ ‖E‖<sub>F</sub>²/(2(1 − ‖E‖)). Pinsker and Bretagnolle–Huber turn KL into TV. No expansion in the
        displacement is made.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">C · a certificate from a phase envelope</span>
        <h3>The whole image given the room’s largest phase</h3>
        <p>If ‖C₀<sup>−½</sup>ΔA‖<sub>F</sub> ≤ q, ρ = 2q + q² bounds ‖E‖ exactly (E = DB* + BD* + DD*), so KL ≤ ρ²/(2(1 − ρ)) and TV ≤ min(√(KL/2), √(1 − e<sup>−KL</sup>)). A phase-only change
        obeys |e<sup>−iΦ</sup> − 1|² ≤ Φ², so q² ≤ Σ|T₀|²Φ² / λ<sub>min</sub>(C₀) ≤ m P²/λ<sub>min</sub>: the peak phase P at every one of the image’s
        {' '}{(fc.geometry.pixel_count / 1e9).toFixed(2)} billion pixels, over its {(fc.geometry.in_band_bins / 1e6).toFixed(0)} million retained bins. A background
        motion bounded the same way proves λ<sub>min</sub>(C₀) ≥ [max(0, 1 − √Q<sub>bg</sub>)]² (plus the receiver noise’s
        variance, where it is counted) = {fc.background_included.covariance_floor_proved_within_model.toFixed(4)}.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">C′ · one line at a time</span>
        <h3>A valid reference under any shaking</h3>
        <p>Widen the image’s range band to every bin (the real image is a function of the widened one, so this can only add
        information): each along-track line is then independent, and KL adds over lines. On each line, C₀ = B₀B₀* + σ²I with
        B₀ = E∘e<sup>−iφ₀</sup>, E orthonormal, so λ<sub>min</sub>(C₀) ≥ σ² + [max(0, 1 − ‖B₀ − E‖<sub>F</sub>)]² (where the
        motion’s term fails, the receiver noise alone still floors it) with that line’s own motion in B₀: the lorry’s wave, the ground’s trembling at its worst realisation, world 0’s own cavity. The whole
        image’s floor failed under the lorry (its common phase energy over 1.3 billion pixels exceeds one); one line’s is
        {bmFloor != null ? ` at least ${bmFloor.toFixed(2)}` : ' near one'}. Theorem C on each line, summed, bounds every
        reader of the one image. Tested against the exact divergence on random lines and on the benchmark’s own
        ({bmVer != null ? `${bmVer.toFixed(2)} of the certificate` : '—'}).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">D · the oracle</span>
        <h3>Told everything but the answer</h3>
        <p>Given the reflectivity, the background motion, the shaking and both models, the raw echoes are Gaussian with the receiver
        noise’s covariance and means that differ by the echo of the room-specific motion alone: TV = erf(√d/2) exactly, d the
        noise-normalised energy of that difference. Averaged over what the oracle was told (only independent uniform phases
        needed), TV ≤ erf(√(E d)/2); the background motion cancels; 95% at 5% needs E d ≥ {fc.requirements.oracle_energy_necessary_for_tv_090.toFixed(2)}.
        Checked against pulse-by-pulse echoes on the real dwell: {orc.raw_echo_check.summary.background_x1.mean.toFixed(3)} ± {orc.raw_echo_check.summary.background_x1.se.toFixed(3)} of the formula.
        The bound is on the average over scenes; for one scene, by Markov’s inequality, d ≤ t E d with probability at least
        1 − 1/t (the spread measured on fixed scenes is a few per cent, P2-29, P2-36). Every realisation of a random
        excitation, rather than its expectation, costs at most √(2n) in amplitude for n equally loud independent directions.
        A mixture of alternatives (any prior over rooms and layouts) is no easier to detect than the prior’s average of its
        members, TV(P₀, Σ wᵢPᵢ) ≤ Σ wᵢ TV(P₀, Pᵢ): a bound that holds for every member holds for the mixture, while one hard
        member does not make a mixture hard (one hard pair defeats a uniform guarantee, not every prior). N images,
        where their independence is justified, raise the divergence N times, so the ceiling √(KL/2) grows at most √N
        times; the TV itself need not follow that ceiling.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">E · depth</span>
        <h3>Two worlds a method cannot tell apart</h3>
        <p>For two admissible worlds at depths Δ apart, any depth estimate has equal-prior mean absolute error at least
        (Δ/2)(1 − TV). One indistinguishable pair is enough to rule out reliable depth over any class that contains it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">and the tight layer</span>
        <h3>What speckle adds</h3>
        <p>For speckle, motion shared by the scene leaves the image’s distribution unchanged, so only what differs from place
        to place counts: the Fisher information F = Σ|Φ̃(f − f′; f) − Φ̃(f − f′; f′)|², with a power-series remainder ε bounding
        the rest, ‖E‖ ≤ √F + ε (P2-25). This is far tighter than the certificate, and assumes the reference covariance is the
        identity, where the certificate proves its floor with the background in it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Theorems A–E are from an independent derivation made for this lab and checked in its code (<span class="mono">sim/sarsim/finite.py</span>,
      with its tests); the tight layer is <span class="mono">sim/sarsim/information.py</span>. Background:
      {' '}<a href="https://web.stanford.edu/class/stats311/lecture-notes.pdf">Duchi’s lecture notes</a> on total variation, data processing,
      Pinsker and two-point testing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="claims">
    <h2>The question is broader than detection: presence, horizontal location, shape, relative vertical geometry and absolute depth are scored apart</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Does one complex SAR acquisition hold reproducible information about what is actually underground, where it is or its
      shape, that survives controls and predicts withheld ground, even if its depth stays uncertain? A method that drew a
      branching tunnel or a room’s layout at one of two possible depths would be a major success, so an uncalibrated or
      aliased depth scale does not by itself dismiss a method. Each claim is judged on its own evidence.
    </p>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>claim</th><th>what bounds or tests it</th><th>where it stands</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Presence: a room here or not</td><td>the three layers below, which bound every processor within the model</td><td>under the ground’s own trembling, no useful detection ({pctp(micro.tpr_upper_at_fpr_005, 2)} found at 5% false alarms, at most, for the bench room); with a lorry beside a room under a 5 m roof, one image lets any method find at most {found(bm.strong['presence (room)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} at 5% false alarms (an L tunnel {found(bm.strong['presence (L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)}{bm.strong['presence (10 m room)'] ? `, a 10 m room ${found(bm.strong['presence (10 m room)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)}` : ''}); a detector told every reflectivity, at the best specified noise floor, {found(bm.strong['presence (room)'].oracle[bmN].tv_upper)} (the benchmark); the structures claimed under Khafre, computed, {found(clL1('claim (both)'))} (P2-38)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Horizontal location</td><td>the same bounds: telling a room here from a room there is a test between two worlds, and TV(A, B) ≤ TV(A, none) + TV(none, B)</td><td>bounded wherever presence is; in the benchmark, the room or the same room 6 m east, with a lorry beside it: no test between the two places finds the right one more than {found(bm.strong['location (6 m)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} of the time at 5% false alarms, and with the two equally likely no method names the right place more than {half(bmBand('location (6 m)'))} of the time (½ + TV/2); any estimate’s mean error over the pair is at least {(3 * (1 - bm.strong['location (6 m)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)).toFixed(1)} m of the 3 m that guessing leaves; in the shape test (P2-31, below), where the data hold a layout (amplified to 20 rad) a reference detector told the shaking places it {m1(stPos?.reference.median_location_error_m)} from its centre, but at 2 rad cannot find it by scanning; the published picture read blind centres {m1(stPos?.published_blind.median_centroid_error_m)} off even at 20 rad, the controls {m1(stNum('control_centroid_error_blind_m'))}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shape: a room, an L-shaped or a branching tunnel</td><td>the same inequality between any two layouts; the shape test (P2-31) on physically generated scenes, scored blind</td><td>bounded under the ground’s own trembling (P2-32); in the benchmark, the room or the L tunnel with a lorry beside them, one image at most {found(bm.strong['shape (room or L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} at 5% false alarms; in the shape test the reference detector names {pc0(stPos?.reference.shape_accuracy)} of layouts at 20 rad and {pc0(stMid?.reference.shape_accuracy)} at 2 rad when told where to look; the published picture read blind {named(stPos?.published_blind ?? ({} as StR))} at 20 rad, chance at the real level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Relative vertical geometry</td><td>whether deeper parts are drawn deeper, whatever the scale</td><td>not shown: the published axis is the registration’s frequency (P2-06, P2-28)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Absolute depth</td><td>the axis’s calibration, and whether different depths leave different marks</td><td>uncalibrated and aliased in the published method; physically, in this model, the mark’s shape changes with depth (below), so the physics does not rule depth out; but detecting a room does not imply telling its depth, and each pair of depths is bounded on its own (Theorem E, P2-29, P2-30)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      A bound on detection rate − false-alarm rate is a bound at every operating point: found ≤ false alarms + TV. Under the
      regional microseism envelope that leaves no prospecting value within the model, even for ranking sites (TV ≤ {sci(micro.tv_upper)});
      where the upper bound is large, as for strong shaking close by, the argument does not exclude prospecting value short of
      95% at 5%; that is not evidence that any method has it, which only an implemented detector succeeding on independent
      tests would show. The defensible conclusion is that useful recovery is excluded under these explicitly bounded
      conditions, not that vibration-based mapping from one image can never work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2>What stays open: bright persistent ground beside strong shaking, the far field, the physical envelopes, the noise floor, and measurement</h2>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>open</th><th>why</th><th>what would close or open it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bright persistent ground beside strong shaking: an important unresolved case within the benchmark</td><td>at {bmTop} dB, which stands for a building, a corner reflector or the pyramid’s edge, the oracle’s bound is {tvTxt(Math.min(...bmOrTop))} to {tvTxt(Math.max(...bmOrTop))} for every strong pair, and the speckle layer does not apply there: a shallow room beside a lorry under a bright persistent target is excluded by no layer of this benchmark</td><td>a diffuse-plus-bright-scatterer model fitted to the image itself, with Khafre’s geometry and the specified noise</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The rest of the real acquisition</td><td>the benchmark is one geology and five shallow layouts in fully developed speckle; a shallow room is not by itself an upper bound on larger or deeper structures; the claim is computed under the ground’s own trembling with the pyramid omitted (its load, coupling and scattering; the direction of its effect not established), its sizes and layout representative, the shafts’ column model matched to the solver empirically</td><td>the pyramid in the model; ground motion measured on the plateau during a pass</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The envelope beyond 70 m</td><td>97–98% of the strong case’s signal is an assumed surface wave carried undiminished to the image’s edge; the measured fall to 70 m supports it, the ×5 test is robustness, not verification</td><td>the scattered field measured to 100 m or more; attenuation measured in the plateau’s rock</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The physical envelopes</td><td>the imprint is the lab’s linear solver in uniform bare rock (P2-04, P2-26) and, for the claim, a damped half-space at 0.2 Hz; the ambient levels are regional; other excitations of the deep structure (a lorry’s body waves, local sources) and nonlinear or parametric coupling are not modelled</td><td>ground motion measured on the plateau during a pass; a proponent’s quantitative prediction of the room-specific motion</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The noise floor</td><td>neither product carries one, and ICEYE’s specification changed between versions (Dwell Fine at best −18 dB in documentation 6.0.0, {String(bmSnr.nesz_headline_db).replace('-', '−')} dB in 6.0.8); every oracle row takes the most favourable, {bmSnrNom.toFixed(1)} dB, the older figure {bmOld != null ? `${bmOld.toFixed(1)} dB` : '—'}; the image’s darkest cells bound the ratio only from below ({bmSnr.measured_floor_lower_db.toFixed(1)} dB)</td><td>the applicable noise for this acquisition from ICEYE, or a noise-only record for the product</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Measured validation</td><td>the geophone-and-satellite comparison (P2-22, P2-23) is simulated</td><td>shaker or reflector data with instruments on them; surveyed voids and matched controls, blind</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="sub">
      A method that succeeds on well-designed, withheld ground would show that the measurements or these inputs deserve revision;
      the bounds are written so such a revision can be made, not to protect a conclusion. The test that would settle it is frozen
      and blind: motion recovered from a measured moving reflector first, then withheld cavities and controls predicted with
      settings fixed in advance (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#settle`}>What would settle it</a>). What would help most from the method’s
      proponents is a predicted room-specific surface motion, with its source, footprint, amplitude and phase, losses and duration,
      which can be put straight into these formulas without releasing any software.
    </p>
  </section>
  <p class="section-kicker" id="detail">In detail: each layer and check, folded</p>

  <section id="shape">
    <h2>Telling one layout from another is bounded the same way, and moving a reconstruction up or down cannot rescue it</h2>
    <details class="fold"><summary>the calculation, in full</summary>
    <p class="sub">
      If two layouts leave nearly the same image in distribution, no algorithm can tell them apart, however it
      reconstructs. A uniformly reliable shape mapper must separate two layouts even after the depth freedom it is allowed;
      the closest admissible pair of depth variants is no easier than the pair at the same depth, so the same-depth bound
      holds for every allowance. Each layout’s imprint is the lab’s solver (statically for the ambient levels, dynamically
      for a lorry close by), and the bound is on the difference between two layouts’ imprints (P2-32).
    </p>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>which of the two?</th><th>tight (microseisms)</th><th>oracle (microseisms)</th><th>oracle’s bound, a lorry beside it all pass</th><th>signal over detection’s</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {sh.static.map((p) => (
          <tr><td>{pairName[p.pair] ?? p.pair}</td><td>{sci(p.rows[0].tight_ceiling)}</td><td>{sci(p.rows[0].oracle_tv)}</td><td>{shDyn(p.pair) ? `${shDyn(p.pair)!.oracle_tv.toFixed(2)} at ${Math.round(shDyn(p.pair)!.worst_f_hz)} Hz` : '—'}</td><td>{p.pair.endsWith('none') ? '1 (detection)' : `${(p.discrimination_signal_factor ?? 0).toFixed(1)}×`}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Detection rate − false-alarm rate, at most. The tight bound and the certificate take each pair through empty ground,
      TV(A, B) ≤ TV(A, none) + TV(none, B), each layout certified against the same reference, since subtracting two layouts’
      motions does not remove the reference covariance; the oracle uses the difference itself, where, told the reflectivity
      and the background, it is exact. The loose certificate with the background caps every pair at
      {' '}{pctp(shMaxCert, 1)} found at 5% false alarms under the microseisms. “Signal over detection’s”: how many times the signal
      that detects the first layout is needed to tell the two apart. Shallow, bench-scale layouts in uniform rock: more
      favourable than anything claimed, and only these.
    </p>
    <h3 class="subhead">{stPos && (stPos.reference.shape_accuracy ?? 0) >= 2 / 3
      ? 'The shape test: where the data hold a layout, a reference detector names and places it; the published picture, read blind, was not shown to do either'
      : 'The shape test: a room, an L-shaped and a branching tunnel generated through the forward model and read blind'}</h3>
    <p class="sub">
      Each layout’s imprint is the lab’s solver; the image is the synthesizer’s, with the unrelated ground and the shaking
      matched across layouts and controls (no cavity, and a motionless copy), three realisations each (P2-31). The
      reference detector is told the shaking: it names a layout by its statistic at the true place and places it by
      scanning every placement. The published method’s plan map is read blind; its change against the same ground without
      the cavity is a diagnostic it never has. Amplified levels are diagnostics, not predictions.
    </p>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>imprint</th><th>reference: names</th><th>reference: scan peaks from the centre</th><th>our implementation of the published method, blind: names</th><th>its top places from the centre</th><th>published change (diagnostic): names</th><th>its top places</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {stLevels.map(({ label, v }) => (
          <tr><td>{label}</td><td>{named(v.reference)}</td><td>{m1(v.reference.median_location_error_m)}</td><td>{named(v.published_blind)}</td><td>{m1(v.published_blind.median_centroid_error_m)}</td><td>{named(v.published_paired)}</td><td>{m1(v.published_paired.median_centroid_error_m)}</td></tr>
        ))}
        <tr><td>chance (the controls)</td><td>33%</td><td>{m1(stNum('control_reference_peak_distance_m'))}</td><td>33%</td><td>{m1(stNum('control_centroid_error_blind_m'))}</td><td>33%</td><td>{m1(stNum('control_centroid_error_paired_m'))}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Nine images per row: three layouts on each of three independently drawn grounds; the three grounds are the independent
      units, and the layouts on one ground are paired comparisons. Distances are medians; p is a null significance test, the
      true layouts permuted within each ground over every arrangement, shown where below 0.2 and not corrected for the dozen
      comparisons; it is not a confidence interval for performance. The paired chance is the motionless
      copy’s change (the no-cavity image’s change against itself is zero). {stPos && stMid && stMid.reference.median_presence_z_at_true_place != null
        ? `At 2 rad the reference detector’s response at the true place stands a median ${stMid.reference.median_presence_z_at_true_place.toFixed(1)} spreads above the scatter over placements: enough to name a layout when told where to look, not enough to find it by scanning the image.`
        : ''} {stPos && stNum('control_depth_error_after_allowance_m') != null
        ? `Depth, for the published change after the predeclared family of transformations (12 candidate depths): ${m1((stPos as StLvD).published_depth_error_after_allowance_m)} from the truth at 20 rad, ${m1(stNum('control_depth_error_after_allowance_m'))} for the motionless copy; closeness alone says little.`
        : ''}
    </p>
      </details>
  </section>

  <section id="boundary">
    <h2>The boundary: the ground’s own trembling rules out useful recovery within the model, and in the benchmark so does a lorry beside a shallow room for one image; a reference image at high SNR, bright persistent points and unspecified mechanisms stay open</h2>
    <details class="fold"><summary>the calculation, in full</summary>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>conditions</th><th>presence</th><th>location and shape</th><th>absolute depth</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Giza’s regional trembling, any reader, one image</td><td>excluded: ≤ {pctp(micro.tpr_upper_at_fpr_005, 2)} found at 5% (certificate), {sci(orReg)} for the oracle</td><td>excluded: each pair ≤ {pctp(shMaxCert, 1)} at 5%</td><td>excluded with presence; aliased in the published method</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The noisiest ground on Earth</td><td>excluded (tight {sci(s0[1].ceiling)})</td><td>excluded with presence</td><td>excluded with presence</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A lorry beside a room all pass, known exactly, one image</td><td>excluded in the benchmark: ≤ {tvTxt(bm.strong['presence (room)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} for a room under a 5 m roof ({bm.strong['presence (room)'].l1[bmN].state}), ≤ {tvTxt(bm.strong['presence (L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} for an L tunnel ({bm.strong['presence (L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].state}); the scattered wave carried to the image’s edge undiminished</td><td>excluded in the benchmark: ≤ {tvTxt(bm.strong['location (6 m)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} (6 m), ≤ {tvTxt(bm.strong['shape (room or L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} (shape)</td><td>about as hard as detection in this model ({orc.depth.signal_factor_depth_over_detection.toFixed(1)}× its signal, P2-29)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The same, told every scatterer’s reflectivity (the oracle)</td><td>{tvTxt(bm.strong['presence (room)'].oracle[bmN].tv_upper)} at the best specified {bmSnrNom.toFixed(1)} dB; its bound stops excluding only at {Math.round(bm.strong['presence (room)'].oracle_bound_stops_excluding_at_snr_db ?? 0)} dB; P2-29’s plane wave at the worst of four sides gives {tvTxt(orThin?.tv_upper)} under a 5 m roof, {tvTxt(orBenchTruck?.tv_upper)} for the bench room. Upper bounds, not detections</td><td>{tvTxt(bm.strong['location (6 m)'].oracle[bmN].tv_upper)} and {tvTxt(bm.strong['shape (room or L tunnel)'].oracle[bmN].tv_upper)}; {shWorstDyn ? `${shWorstDyn.oracle_tv.toFixed(2)} for P2-32’s hardest pair` : ''}</td><td>not computed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Any mechanism with gain, not yet specified</td><td colspan="3">not covered: it must state the motion it adds over the room (source, footprint, amplitude and phase, losses, duration); the requirement curve above says how much is needed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card"><span class="tag">achieved, conditionally</span><h3>Limits within a stated measurement and physical model</h3><p>The theorems and the three layers above: finite, algorithm-independent, and scoped to fully developed speckle (or any texture, for the oracle), the Doppler-to-time relation, the lab’s linear elastic imprints and the regional ambient levels.</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><span class="tag planned">a research goal</span><h3>Limits for a defined class of realistic archaeological settings</h3><p>Feasible, with stronger physical validation: measured ground motion at the site during a pass, measured coupling over known voids, a qualified observation model and noise floor, and the open regime examined by experiment rather than by bound.</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><span class="tag claimed">not claimed</span><h3>That every conceivable single-image application has no archaeological value</h3><p>Too broad for this work: mechanisms, environments and acquisitions outside the model are not covered, and the open regime is open. The outcome sought is this boundary, not a verdict fixed in advance.</p></div>
    </div>
      </details>
  </section>

  <section id="layers">
    <h2>Three layers, one answer for the ground’s own trembling; under strong shaking the whole-image references fail, and the benchmark’s per-line certificate replaces them</h2>
    <details class="fold"><summary>the calculation, in full</summary>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>shaking</th><th>level</th><th>certificate (P2-30)</th><th>tight (P2-25)</th><th>oracle (P2-29)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {layers.map((l) => (
          <tr><td>{l.what}</td><td>{l.level}</td><td>{sci(l.cert)}<br /><span class="dim">{l.certNote}</span></td><td>{l.tight === null ? '—' : sci(l.tight)}<br /><span class="dim">{l.tightNote}</span></td><td>{tvTxt(l.oracle)}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Each entry bounds detection rate − false-alarm rate; with two equally likely cases the best accuracy exceeds a coin toss
      by half of it. The certificate and the tight bound hold within fully developed speckle and the Doppler-to-time relation
      (checked pulse by pulse, P2-25); the oracle holds for any texture with independent phases, at {orc.manifest.params.snr_db.toFixed(1)} dB
      per resolution cell (the site’s median backscatter measured in the image over the best noise floor ICEYE specifies
      for the product’s mode, {String(orc.manifest.params.nesz_best_db).replace('-', '−')} dB in documentation 6.0.8: a scenario, not a measurement).
      The ambient levels are regional, not upper limits at the pyramids. Under traffic or a lorry the ground without a room
      already moves with the source, so these rows’ certificate and tight bound (no such motion in the reference) are
      restricted surrogates, not bounds; the whole image gives no floor with the lorry’s wave in it, but one along-track
      line does, and the benchmark above bounds a single image that way. The oracle, whose mean echoes differ only by the
      added motion, stays valid, with the scattered wave carried undiminished across the scene at the worst of four sampled
      sides and the grid checks’ allowances. On small test images the best test comes within {' '}{detOver.toFixed(1)} times of the tight
      bound: these are bounds, not what a detector achieves.
    </p>
      </details>
  </section>

  <section id="mechanism">
    <h2>What a mechanism must supply: a large enough displacement integral over the ground, whatever its pattern; smooth patterns need far more</h2>
    <details class="fold"><summary>the calculation, in full</summary>
    <p class="sub">
      Corrected on 30 September after two independent reviews. The solid curves were first described as the motion any
      mechanism must supply; they hold only for the smooth pattern computed. The requirement for every pattern is on the
      displacement integral <i>E</i> = ∫ max<sub>t</sub> |Δ<i>u</i><sub>los</sub>|² d<i>A</i> (m⁴: an integrated squared
      displacement envelope, not a mechanical energy). Under strong shaking the whole-image certificate has no valid reference (the ground
      without a room already moves with the source), so its curve applies to quiet ground; the oracle’s applies under any
      shaking, and the benchmark above bounds a single image under a lorry line by line.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="The least room-specific line-of-sight motion for 95% found at 5% false alarms, against footprint"
        series={[
          ...rqMain.filter((r) => r.f_hz === 0.2 || r.f_hz === 80).map((r, i) => ({ name: `${r.f_hz} Hz, smooth pattern`, x: r.exact.map((e) => e.area_half_m2), y: r.exact.map((e) => e.D_necessary_m['0.9']), color: rqColours[i === 0 ? 0 : 4], width: 1.8 })),
          { name: 'any pattern, oracle', x: anyCurve(Eor).map((c) => c.A), y: anyCurve(Eor).map((c) => c.D), color: 'var(--text)', dash: '2 3', width: 1.3 },
          { name: 'any pattern, certificate (quiet)', x: anyCurve(Emin).map((c) => c.A), y: anyCurve(Emin).map((c) => c.D), color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '4 3', width: 1.3 },
        ]}
        xLabel="footprint, m²"
        yLabel="peak displacement, m, line of sight"
        logX
        logY
        yFormat="exp"
        height={240}
        caption="Solid: exact within the model for one smooth (Gaussian) pattern (P2-27), at 0.2 and 80 Hz; 2, 5 and 20 Hz lie between them in the run (20 Hz almost on the 80 Hz line). Dotted (the oracle with receiver noise, valid under any shaking) and dashed (the certificate with Giza’s background, quiet ground only): every pattern and time history, by the displacement integral, drawn as the temporal peak displacement of a motion filling the footprint evenly (a spatial rms of temporal peaks), the same at every frequency. Below a line the bound excludes 95% at 5%; between a dotted line and a solid one only the smooth pattern is excluded. Crossing a line shows neither detectability nor usefulness."
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">conditional</span>
        <h3>Phase modulation, nonlinear coupling, anything</h3>
        <p>The bounds use the full complex change of the image, phase included; no spectral peak is needed. Any mechanism,
        linear or not, reaches one image only through the motion it adds. An oracle told the reflectivity, the excitation and
        both models, with receiver noise at the best specified {(rq.any_pattern?.oracle_snr_db ?? 0).toFixed(1)} dB per cell, cannot reach 95% at 5% while that
        motion’s displacement integral stays below {Eor != null ? `${sci(Eor)} m⁴` : 'the boundary'}, whatever its pattern
        or time history, and whatever motion both worlds share, which cancels from its mean echoes; below
        {' '}{Eor05 != null ? `${sci(Eor05)} m⁴` : '—'} it stays within five points of chance. Every method told less
        inherits this. Under quiet ground the speckle certificate, with Giza’s background in the reference, gives
        {' '}{Emin != null ? `${sci(Emin)} m⁴` : '—'} without assuming receiver noise.</p>
        <p>The pattern matters as well as the size. {pat02 ? `A Gaussian 4 m wide at 0.2 Hz, multiplied by a cosine of 4 m period along track, holds ${pat02.ratio_to_gaussian['cos along track, 4 m period'].toFixed(0)} times its information though nowhere larger (${pat02.ratio_to_gaussian['cos along track, 1 m period'].toFixed(0)} times for a 1 m period). The Gaussian’s information is ${sci(pat02.gaussian_share_of_envelope_bound)} of the general upper bound for its envelope, 4N⟨Φ²⟩, which is a bound, not an attainable optimum; at 80 Hz it is ${pat80 ? (100 * pat80.gaussian_share_of_envelope_bound).toFixed(0) : '—'}%.` : ''}</p>
        <p>A change of phase alone moves the ground by at most twice the motion it acts on, 2<i>a</i>. To escape this
        exclusion, under its stated amplitude and noise, such a mechanism must change the phase over more than
        {' '}<i>E</i><sub>min</sub>/(2<i>a</i>)²{phaseArea != null ? `: ${Math.round(phaseArea).toLocaleString('en-US')} m² under Giza’s regional microseisms, a disk of radius ${Math.round(Math.sqrt(phaseArea / Math.PI))} m` : ''}.
        That is necessary to escape this particular bound, not sufficient for detection. Whether a physically justified
        cavity-induced field, tails included, reaches the threshold is the table’s question below. A mechanism with gain is
        not excluded: it needs its output specified (source, footprint, amplitude and phase, losses, duration) before it can
        be tested.</p>
        <details class="calculator" data-lambda={fc.geometry.wavelength_m} data-density={fc.geometry.area_density_used}>
          <summary>Try an envelope in the certificate</summary>
          <div class="inputs">
            <label>Footprint (m²)<input name="area" type="number" value="1000" min="0.001" step="100" /></label>
            <label>Peak room-specific motion (µm)<input name="displacement" type="number" value="0.12" min="0" step="0.01" /></label>
            <label>Covariance floor<input name="floor" type="number" value="1" min="0.000001" step="0.1" /></label>
          </div>
          <p><output aria-live="polite"></output></p>
          <p class="dim">The loose certificate (Theorem C): zero-mean Gaussian texture, phase-only change, continuum area. Inputs are assumptions, not measured motion.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>the bench room’s modelled imprint</th><th>short of a smooth pattern of its peak and footprint</th><th>short of the oracle’s requirement, any pattern</th><th>short of the certificate’s (quiet ground only)</th><th>oracle’s bound on found − false alarms</th><th>outcome</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rq.imprints.map((im) => (
          <tr><td>{im.label.replace('bench room, ', '').replace(', measured', '').replace('The world', 'the world')}{im.label.includes('dynamic') ? `, ${Math.round(im.f_hz)} Hz` : ''}</td><td>{sci(im.short_by)}</td><td>{shortTxt(im.short_by_oracle)}{im.short_by_oracle_near_only != null ? <span class="dim"><br />within 39 m only: {shortTxt(im.short_by_oracle_near_only)}</span> : ''}</td><td>{im.short_by_certificate != null ? shortTxt(im.short_by_certificate) : '— (no valid reference under the lorry)'}</td><td>{tvTxt(im.oracle_tv_upper)}</td><td>{im.outcome ?? '—'}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      “Short” is how many times the imprint’s amplitude would have to grow before the bound stops excluding 95% at 5%. At every
      operating point found ≤ false alarms + the bound: below 0.05 the reader is within five points of chance; below 0.9,
      95% at 5% is excluded but weaker, possibly useful performance is not; at 0.9 or more the bound does not decide
      (unresolved), which is not a demonstrated detection. The static imprint is bounded over every strain direction and
      beyond the solver’s grid by Eshelby’s void as Okada point sources, scaled to the solver{rq.static_integral ? ` (${Math.max(...rq.static_integral.solver_over_analytic_largest).toFixed(2)} times at most inside the grid, times ${rq.static_integral.envelope_margin})` : ''},
      with the grid check’s 10% and a random field’s peak (3/√2 times its rms amplitude). The dynamic imprint counts the whole
      39 m disk, untapered, at the worst of four sampled incidence sides (others are not computed), and beyond it the scattered
      wave by energy-flux conservation for a surface wave without attenuation, to the scene’s farthest corner, with the grid
      check’s allowance. Under the lorry the margin rests on how far the scattered wave carries: counted within 39 m it is
      several times larger.
    </p>
      </details>
  </section>

  <section id="depth">
    <h2>{orc.depth.residual_fraction > 0.3 ? `Depth is carried by the mark’s shape in this model: rooms spread 15 m down leave ${Math.round(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction)}% of a deeper room’s mark unmatched; the published method’s axis does not use it` : `Absolute depth is the hardest claim: rooms spread 15 m down match a deeper room’s mark to within ${(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction).toFixed(1)}%`}</h2>
    <details class="fold"><summary>the calculation, in full</summary>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">any method · P2-29, P2-30</span>
        <h3>An obstruction sought, and not found</h3>
        <p>Could shallower voids mimic a deeper one? Small rooms 15 m down, spread over the ground in any non-negative mix, were
        fitted to the surface mark of the bench room 30 m down on the lab’s solver (two solved together agree with their sum to
        {' '}{(100 * orc.depth.pair_interaction_fraction).toFixed(1)}%): {Math.round(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction)}% of the mark is left unmatched, and the bench room
        15 m down alone leaves {Math.round(100 * orc.depth.best_single_bench_room_residual)}%. In uniform rock the mark changes shape with depth, not only size, so telling these
        depths apart takes only {orc.depth.signal_factor_depth_over_detection.toFixed(1)} times the signal detection takes.
        {fc.depth_oracle && ` Theorem E's floor on any method's mean depth error, where detection just reaches 95% at 5%, is then only ${fc.depth_oracle.rows[0].mean_depth_error_lower_m.toFixed(1)} m of their ${fc.depth_oracle.separation_m} m separation.`}
        {' '}Richer classes (layered rock, fractures, voids of other shapes) could still mimic one another; that is not tested.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">the published method · P2-28</span>
        <h3>Its depth follows the shaking, not the room</h3>
        <p>Run whole over the bench room 15 m down, the same room 30 m down and a granite block, each imprint boosted to 2 rad, the
        published focusing spreads each change over its whole axis: {pctp(d15.share_12_18_m, 0)} of the 15 m room’s change lies between 12 and 18 m
        and {pctp(d30.share_27_33_m, 0)} of the 30 m room’s between 27 and 33 m, where {pctp(d15.axis_share_12_18_m, 0)} and {pctp(d30.axis_share_27_33_m, 0)} of the axis lie.
        Horizontally its change does gather over the room ({conc(d15).toFixed(1)} times elsewhere), but a random perturbation of the
        same pixels gathers almost as much ({conc(dNull).toFixed(1)} times): in this paired comparison the picture changes where the image
        changes. Its depth coordinate is an uncalibrated focus coordinate, the frequency of the registration series (P2-06): a calibration
        and aliasing problem, which a method that recovered the right layout would survive. It does not recover the layout: in
        the shape test (above) its blind map names a layout no better than guessing even where a reference detector names every one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
      </details>
  </section>

</Doc>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLDetailsElement>('.calculator').forEach((box) => {
    const area = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="area"]')!;
    const disp = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="displacement"]')!;
    const floor = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="floor"]')!;
    const out = box.querySelector<HTMLOutputElement>('output')!;
    const lam = Number(box.dataset.lambda);
    const dens = Number(box.dataset.density);
    const calc = () => {
      const A = area.valueAsNumber;
      const u = disp.valueAsNumber * 1e-6;
      const z = floor.valueAsNumber;
      if (![A, u, z].every(Number.isFinite) || A <= 0 || u < 0 || z <= 0) {
        out.textContent = 'Enter a positive footprint and floor, and a motion of zero or more.';
        return;
      }
      const q = Math.sqrt((dens * A) / z) * ((4 * Math.PI * u) / lam);
      const rho = 2 * q + q * q;
      const kl = rho < 1 ? (rho * rho) / (2 * (1 - rho)) : Infinity;
      const v = Math.min(1, Math.sqrt(kl / 2), Math.sqrt(-Math.expm1(-kl)));
      out.textContent =
        v >= 0.9
          ? 'The certificate does not exclude 95% at 5% for these inputs: unresolved, which does not make detection possible.'
          : `Found ≤ false alarms + ${v.toPrecision(3)}: at 5% false alarms, at most ${(100 * Math.min(1, 0.05 + v)).toFixed(1)}%. ${v < 0.05 ? 'Within five points of chance.' : 'The 95% at 5% target is excluded; a detector could still find up to that much.'}`;
    };
    [area, disp, floor].forEach((el) => el.addEventListener('input', calc));
    calc();
  });
</script>

